  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The offense didn't score until it was far too late.",
    "what_this_means": "Ten runners left on base and thirteen strikeouts define a Sacramento team that couldn't punch through when it mattered most. The Angels scored twice in the second and four more in the sixth while Ginn held the line with seven hits and four earned runs through 5.1 innings. That's a starter good enough to win. The bullpen. Barlow and Hartlieb combining for 3.2 scoreless. Held tight in its own right. But runners on base in the first, fifth, and seventh inning produced nothing, and by the time Sacramento finally scratched across a run, the game was over and the season trending the wrong way. This isn't a one-game problem. This is a team that leaves runners on and walks away empty, then wonders why the margin is closing in the division race. The bottom of the order couldn't carry the weight.",
    "what_to_watch": "Reid Detmers looks like the Angels' answer at 3.68 ERA. Jack Perkins takes the mound tonight for Sacramento with the division already reminding them that half-measures don't survive.",
